WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.300
Why Finpoint?

00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:04.350
Your bank can tell you where every dollar went.

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:07.731
What it can’t tell you is what each payment was really for.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.160
Say you spent $80 at the supermarket.

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:16.600
$50 of that was groceries, and $30 was a birthday gift for a friend.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.450
Your bank shows one $80 payment.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:22.658
But to you, those are two very different things.

00:00:23.533 --> 00:00:29.373
Or you paid $90 for dinner with a friend, and they sent their $60 share back a week later.

00:00:29.833 --> 00:00:32.713
Your bank shows two payments, a week apart:

00:00:32.883 --> 00:00:34.553
money out, and money in.

00:00:34.903 --> 00:00:37.505
What dinner really cost you was $30.

00:00:38.367 --> 00:00:40.197
Finpoint closes that gap.

00:00:40.537 --> 00:00:46.197
It keeps your bank activity exactly as your bank reports it, and lets you explain it in your own words:

00:00:46.457 --> 00:00:48.357
groceries, $50.

00:00:48.597 --> 00:00:50.327
A gift, $30.

00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:52.701
Your share of dinner, $30.

00:00:53.567 --> 00:00:58.217
Once your spending is explained, your charts and budgets finally tell the truth.

00:00:58.567 --> 00:01:01.327
So using Finpoint comes down to three things:

00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:05.905
bring in your bank activity, explain it, and see where your money really went.

00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:09.848
Statements, Records and Allocations

00:01:10.448 --> 00:01:14.668
To do that, Finpoint uses three words, and everything else builds on them:

00:01:14.978 --> 00:01:17.504
Statements, Records and Allocations.

00:01:18.367 --> 00:01:23.217
A Statement is one line of bank activity, exactly as your bank reports it.

00:01:23.527 --> 00:01:26.084
Like that $80 supermarket payment.

00:01:26.967 --> 00:01:29.537
A Record is your explanation of what happened.

00:01:29.877 --> 00:01:32.447
Groceries for $50 is a Record.

00:01:32.737 --> 00:01:35.030
The gift for $30 is another.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:41.390
An Allocation connects a Statement to a Record, and says how much of the Statement belongs there.

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:48.812
Here, $50 of the supermarket payment is allocated to Groceries, and the other $30 to the gift.

00:01:49.667 --> 00:01:50.987
That’s the first pattern:

00:01:51.137 --> 00:01:53.647
one Statement, split into several Records.

00:01:53.927 --> 00:01:56.371
It’s how you untangle a mixed shopping trip.

00:01:57.233 --> 00:01:59.173
The second pattern goes the other way:

00:01:59.493 --> 00:02:02.353
several Statements, combined into one Record.

00:02:02.773 --> 00:02:10.643
Your $90 dinner payment and your friend’s $60 transfer both belong to one Dinner Record, which comes to your real share:

00:02:10.943 --> 00:02:12.015
$30.

00:02:12.867 --> 00:02:15.407
Most of the time, though, it’s one to one.

00:02:15.807 --> 00:02:20.312
A $12 lunch is one Statement, explained by one Lunch Record.

00:02:21.933 --> 00:02:23.453
Trying Finpoint first

00:02:24.053 --> 00:02:26.713
Before you bring in your own bank, you can look around.

00:02:27.193 --> 00:02:32.193
On your first visit, the Dashboard, Finpoint’s home page, offers demo data:

00:02:32.443 --> 00:02:37.490
three accounts and five months of Records, so you can see what a lived-in Finpoint looks like.

00:02:38.367 --> 00:02:44.797
When you’re ready for your own money, reset the demo data from the Data page, and start with your own bank activity.

00:02:45.267 --> 00:02:46.217
That’s next.

00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:49.528
Importing your bank activity

00:02:50.128 --> 00:02:55.898
Banks let you download your activity as a file, called an export, from your bank’s website or app.

00:02:56.258 --> 00:02:59.653
You bring that file into Finpoint, and it reads every line.

00:03:00.533 --> 00:03:10.598
Finpoint reads exports from DBS, OCBC, UOB and Revolut, as a CSV file or an Excel spreadsheet, depending on the bank.

00:03:11.467 --> 00:03:15.627
Each Statement belongs to an Account, one for each of your bank accounts.

00:03:16.067 --> 00:03:27.075
Import your OCBC 360 Account and your UOB savings account, and Finpoint keeps their Statements apart, using the account details inside each file.

00:03:27.933 --> 00:03:31.773
Some exports, like Revolut’s, don’t say which account they came from.

00:03:32.113 --> 00:03:35.648
For those, Finpoint asks you to pick or name the account.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:42.856
Download a CSV or Excel export from your bank, once for each account you want in Finpoint.

00:03:43.567 --> 00:03:48.019
Then open Importer from the sidebar, choose your bank, and add your files.

00:03:48.733 --> 00:03:51.974
Choose Import, and check how many new Statements came in.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:58.420
Your new Statements now wait in Workflows, under Allocate to Records, until you explain them.

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:00.187
That’s where we’ll go next.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:02.792
Workflows

00:04:03.392 --> 00:04:07.002
Workflows is your to-do list, and where you’ll spend most of your time.

00:04:07.472 --> 00:04:14.357
Its first tab, Allocate to Records, lists every Statement that still has money left to explain, newest first.

00:04:15.233 --> 00:04:19.643
When only part of a Statement has been explained, it shows how much is left.

00:04:19.953 --> 00:04:22.673
Once it’s fully explained, it leaves the list.

00:04:22.963 --> 00:04:24.860
An empty list means you’re done.

00:04:25.733 --> 00:04:28.373
The other two tabs keep your imports clean.

00:04:28.773 --> 00:04:37.593
Fix Duplicates catches a payment your bank exported twice, and Replace Pending swaps a payment your bank hadn’t confirmed yet for the confirmed one.

00:04:38.003 --> 00:04:39.024
We’ll get to both.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.100
Two other pages help you look things up.

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:46.010
Statements lists all your bank activity, explained or not.

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:48.280
Records lists your explanations.

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:51.095
Workflows only shows what still needs you.

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:54.464
Explaining your first purchase

00:04:55.064 --> 00:04:57.274
Let’s explain a $12 lunch.

00:04:57.554 --> 00:05:00.994
In Allocate to Records, click the lunch Statement to select it.

00:05:01.334 --> 00:05:04.459
A bar appears at the top, with what you can do next.

00:05:05.167 --> 00:05:06.557
Choose Create Record.

00:05:06.867 --> 00:05:12.172
A new Record opens with the Statement already allocated, and its amount and date filled in.

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:16.790
Give it a title, like Lunch, and pick a Category, like Dining Out.

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:21.573
A Category says what the money was for, and your spending charts are grouped by it.

00:05:22.433 --> 00:05:26.063
Under Analytics you’ll also see Treatment and Monthly Budget.

00:05:26.453 --> 00:05:27.723
Leave those for now:

00:05:27.923 --> 00:05:31.281
your Category fills them in, and we’ll come back to them later.

00:05:32.133 --> 00:05:35.133
Choose Create Record again, at the bottom, to save.

00:05:35.513 --> 00:05:39.193
The lunch Statement leaves the list, because it’s fully explained.

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:42.204
Splitting one payment

00:05:42.804 --> 00:05:46.664
Now the $80 supermarket trip, which was really two things.

00:05:46.974 --> 00:05:49.274
Select it, and choose Create Record.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:55.580
Call it Groceries, and set both the Record’s amount and its Allocation to $50.

00:05:55.990 --> 00:06:00.940
The Record is what you spent, and the Allocation is how much of this payment covers it.

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:04.384
The other $30 stays unexplained for now.

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:09.859
Save, and the supermarket Statement stays in the list, with $30 left.

00:06:10.567 --> 00:06:16.287
Select it again, and create a second Record, Birthday gift, for the $30 left to allocate.

00:06:16.667 --> 00:06:18.870
Finpoint fills that amount in for you.

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:24.694
Save, and the supermarket Statement leaves the list, because it’s now fully explained.

00:06:25.567 --> 00:06:28.547
Now one Statement is explained by two Records:

00:06:28.827 --> 00:06:32.358
$50 of groceries, and a $30 gift.

00:06:33.967 --> 00:06:36.271
Combining payments into one Record

00:06:36.871 --> 00:06:38.261
Now the shared dinner.

00:06:38.581 --> 00:06:40.111
Select both Statements:

00:06:40.281 --> 00:06:44.551
the $90 you paid, and the $60 your friend Sam sent back.

00:06:44.891 --> 00:06:46.201
The bar nets them out:

00:06:46.451 --> 00:06:50.241
$90 out, $60 back, so $30.

00:06:50.967 --> 00:06:53.447
Choose Create Record, and call it Dinner.

00:06:53.867 --> 00:06:58.751
Both Statements are allocated, and the Record comes to $30, your real share.

00:06:59.633 --> 00:07:03.143
Save, and two Statements are explained by a single Record.

00:07:03.543 --> 00:07:08.081
Your spending shows the $30 you really spent, not $90.

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:11.082
Where your numbers come from

00:07:11.682 --> 00:07:14.432
Now let’s see what those explanations buy you.

00:07:14.702 --> 00:07:18.112
With a few Records saved, your Dashboard starts to fill in.

00:07:18.532 --> 00:07:23.273
Every total on it is calculated from Records, never directly from Statements.

00:07:24.133 --> 00:07:27.173
That’s why importing alone doesn’t change your spending.

00:07:27.513 --> 00:07:30.307
A Statement only counts once you’ve explained it.

00:07:31.167 --> 00:07:35.827
And because it uses your explanations, the Dashboard shows what you really spent:

00:07:36.227 --> 00:07:40.953
$30 on dinner, not $90, and groceries apart from the gift.

00:07:41.833 --> 00:07:48.478
Categories this month breaks your spending down by Category, so the Categories you choose shape everything you see here.

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:51.348
Pending Records

00:07:51.948 --> 00:07:55.438
Your Dashboard is only as current as your explanations.

00:07:55.798 --> 00:08:03.523
But sometimes you know about spending before your bank shows all of it, and you’d rather record it now than try to remember it later.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:11.400
Say you paid $200 for two concert tickets, and your friend Jo will pay you back $100 next week.

00:08:11.810 --> 00:08:17.202
You can already create the Record for your $100 share, from the $200 payment.

00:08:18.067 --> 00:08:19.617
The amounts don’t match yet:

00:08:19.807 --> 00:08:22.427
$100 against $200.

00:08:22.787 --> 00:08:28.187
Finpoint warns you, and saves the Record as Pending, which means it isn’t settled yet.

00:08:28.537 --> 00:08:31.085
Your $100 share already counts.

00:08:31.967 --> 00:08:36.207
Pending Records are marked in your Records list, so nothing gets forgotten.

00:08:37.067 --> 00:08:42.117
When Jo’s $100 arrives, select it and choose Allocate to record.

00:08:42.567 --> 00:08:45.509
Finpoint suggests the Pending Record it completes.

00:08:46.367 --> 00:08:48.637
The Record now combines both payments.

00:08:48.927 --> 00:08:52.518
Save, and because the amounts tally, it’s no longer Pending.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:56.150
So a Record is Pending for one of two reasons.

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:59.220
Its amounts don’t tally yet, like the concert.

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:02.290
Or one of its Statements is still Pending at the bank.

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:06.549
Either way, Pending means the Record isn’t fully confirmed yet.

00:09:08.167 --> 00:09:09.407
Pending Statements

00:09:10.007 --> 00:09:12.297
That second kind of Pending starts at the bank.

00:09:12.657 --> 00:09:16.257
A Pending Statement is bank activity that isn’t confirmed yet.

00:09:16.667 --> 00:09:20.797
Some imports, like Revolut’s, bring card payments in as Pending.

00:09:21.157 --> 00:09:26.920
And if your bank takes days to show a payment, you can add one yourself, so your month stays complete.

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:31.020
On the Statements page, choose Create Pending Statement.

00:09:31.390 --> 00:09:35.009
Pick the account, then enter the amount and a short description.

00:09:35.733 --> 00:09:38.313
It appears in Workflows, marked Pending.

00:09:38.703 --> 00:09:40.593
Explain it like any other Statement:

00:09:40.723 --> 00:09:43.693
here, a burger with Mia, under Dining Out.

00:09:44.073 --> 00:09:48.884
Save, and the Record counts straight away, but stays Pending while its Statement does.

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:54.470
When the confirmed Statement arrives in a later import, open Replace Pending.

00:09:54.950 --> 00:10:00.103
Pick the Pending Statement, and Finpoint suggests the imported Statements it could become.

00:10:00.833 --> 00:10:03.993
Review the swap, and choose Replace pending Statement.

00:10:04.413 --> 00:10:10.803
The Allocations move to the confirmed Statement, the Pending one disappears, and nothing is counted twice.

00:10:12.433 --> 00:10:14.202
Fixing duplicate Statements

00:10:14.802 --> 00:10:17.122
Imports can go wrong in one more way.

00:10:17.452 --> 00:10:21.372
Sometimes a bank changes a payment’s description between exports.

00:10:21.732 --> 00:10:27.293
Import both files, and the same payment arrives twice, so you might explain it twice.

00:10:28.167 --> 00:10:34.747
Finpoint spots Statements with the same account, day and amount, and marks them as possible duplicates.

00:10:35.167 --> 00:10:38.219
Open Fix Duplicates in Workflows to review them.

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:42.270
If they really are two payments, choose Not duplicates.

00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:46.345
Otherwise, choose the Statement to keep, then Review deletion.

00:10:47.067 --> 00:10:50.287
Finpoint shows what will happen before anything is deleted.

00:10:50.697 --> 00:10:55.089
Allocations follow the Statement you keep, so your Records stay explained.

00:10:56.700 --> 00:10:58.422
Finding and fixing Records

00:10:59.022 --> 00:11:03.492
Sooner or later you’ll want to look something up, like what that $90 dinner was.

00:11:03.862 --> 00:11:04.992
Open Records.

00:11:05.272 --> 00:11:09.361
Search by title, or filter by date, Category or amount.

00:11:10.233 --> 00:11:17.224
Open a Record to see its Statements, then choose Edit Record to change any field, or the Allocations behind it.

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:23.608
Statements works the same way for your bank activity, and each Account has a page of its own.

00:11:24.467 --> 00:11:31.185
And if you always look at the same period, set default filters in Settings, so every list opens on that range.

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:33.732
Categories

00:11:34.332 --> 00:11:36.652
We’ve been picking Categories all along.

00:11:37.022 --> 00:11:39.782
They’re the most important choice you make on a Record:

00:11:40.052 --> 00:11:44.048
they say what it was for, and the Dashboard breaks your spending down by them.

00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:47.430
Lunch and dinner with friends are Dining Out.

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:50.000
The supermarket run is Groceries.

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:53.330
The birthday gift goes under Gifts &amp; Donations.

00:11:53.740 --> 00:12:02.118
Finpoint starts you with a sensible set, and you can rename them, add your own, or nest one under another, like Coffee under Dining Out.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.760
Each Category also carries two defaults:

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:08.100
a Treatment, and a Monthly Budget.

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:11.360
That’s why you could leave those fields alone earlier:

00:12:11.470 --> 00:12:13.020
the Category fills them in.

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:16.407
We’ll look at both, right after one more time-saver.

00:12:18.033 --> 00:12:19.188
Statement rules

00:12:19.788 --> 00:12:22.498
Your Categories already fill in two fields.

00:12:22.748 --> 00:12:27.898
Some payments come back every month, like your phone bill, and typing them out again gets old.

00:12:28.358 --> 00:12:33.108
A Statement rule recognises them, and fills in the Record for you when you create it.

00:12:33.468 --> 00:12:35.498
It never creates Records on its own.

00:12:36.367 --> 00:12:38.447
Open Rules and choose New rule.

00:12:38.747 --> 00:12:41.927
Give it a name, and the text to look for in the description.

00:12:42.307 --> 00:12:44.780
Finpoint shows the Statements it matches.

00:12:45.500 --> 00:12:47.040
Then choose what it fills in:

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:50.940
a title, a Category, even People or a Location.

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:53.960
Autofill puts the value straight into the field.

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:57.682
Suggest offers it as a one-click suggestion instead.

00:12:58.533 --> 00:13:06.173
Next time you create a Record from a matching Statement, the autofilled fields are ready, and the suggestions are one click away.

00:13:06.613 --> 00:13:08.341
You can still change any of them.

00:13:09.967 --> 00:13:10.849
Treatments

00:13:11.449 --> 00:13:13.039
Now, those two defaults.

00:13:13.229 --> 00:13:14.549
The first is Treatment.

00:13:14.809 --> 00:13:17.199
It decides how the Dashboard counts a Record:

00:13:17.419 --> 00:13:21.068
as income, as spending, as saving, or not at all.

00:13:21.933 --> 00:13:23.513
Your salary is Income.

00:13:23.803 --> 00:13:25.983
Lunch and groceries are Spending.

00:13:26.363 --> 00:13:29.953
A refund is Spending too, because it lowers what you spent.

00:13:30.343 --> 00:13:35.183
Putting $500 into an investment account is Saving / investment.

00:13:35.463 --> 00:13:40.382
And moving money between two accounts you track is a Transfer, which isn’t counted at all.

00:13:41.233 --> 00:13:45.363
On the Dashboard, Income and Spending make up your totals and charts.

00:13:45.743 --> 00:13:52.493
Saving and investment are kept out of them, in their own section further down, so saving never looks like spending.

00:13:52.853 --> 00:13:54.739
And Transfers don’t appear at all.

00:13:55.600 --> 00:13:57.780
Your Category sets the treatment for you.

00:13:58.090 --> 00:14:01.997
Change it on a single Record only when that Record is an exception.

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:04.936
Monthly Budgets

00:14:05.536 --> 00:14:07.796
The second default is a Monthly Budget.

00:14:08.056 --> 00:14:09.696
It answers a different question:

00:14:09.856 --> 00:14:11.066
am I on track?

00:14:11.266 --> 00:14:14.006
A Monthly Budget is a group of spending you plan for.

00:14:14.346 --> 00:14:16.106
Finpoint starts you with four:

00:14:16.286 --> 00:14:23.216
Daily for everyday spending, Recurring for bills like rent and your phone, Irregular for one-off costs, and Travel.

00:14:23.656 --> 00:14:25.892
Each gets its own amount every month.

00:14:26.767 --> 00:14:28.597
Daily is the one to watch.

00:14:28.937 --> 00:14:35.547
It holds everyday spending, like food and transport, and the Dashboard forecasts where it’s heading by the end of the month.

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:43.330
To give Daily an amount, choose Edit beside it on the Dashboard, and set a Monthly amount, say $500.

00:14:44.033 --> 00:14:47.263
The Amount rule decides which months that amount covers.

00:14:47.603 --> 00:14:50.383
Override one month changes this month only.

00:14:50.723 --> 00:14:53.661
Override default applies from this month onward.

00:14:54.533 --> 00:14:58.053
The Dashboard then shows how the month is going against the plan.

00:14:58.393 --> 00:15:01.413
A Monthly Budget doesn’t move or set aside any money.

00:15:01.773 --> 00:15:03.931
It’s a comparison, nothing more.

00:15:05.533 --> 00:15:06.848
Reading the Overview

00:15:07.448 --> 00:15:12.698
With a plan in place, the Overview, the Dashboard’s first tab, becomes your monthly check-in.

00:15:13.078 --> 00:15:15.268
Its top answers the big questions:

00:15:15.518 --> 00:15:19.428
what came in, what you spent, and your surplus, which is the difference.

00:15:19.878 --> 00:15:24.195
Each number is compared with the last two months, at the same point in the month.

00:15:25.067 --> 00:15:29.267
Choose a Monthly Budget at the top, and the spending charts narrow to it.

00:15:29.657 --> 00:15:34.584
Cards whose names start with Total ignore that choice, and always show everything.

00:15:35.467 --> 00:15:43.334
The Spending forecast chart shows this month so far, where it’s heading, and the months before, so you can see early if you’re running ahead.

00:15:44.200 --> 00:15:51.388
Further down, Categories this month and their trend show what changed, and Biggest changes calls out the largest moves.

00:15:52.267 --> 00:15:55.167
The calendar colours each day by how much you spent.

00:15:55.447 --> 00:15:57.172
Pick a day to see its Records.

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:00.137
Monthly Records

00:16:00.737 --> 00:16:05.527
When a number on the Overview surprises you, Monthly Records shows what’s behind it.

00:16:05.937 --> 00:16:07.607
It sits next to the Overview:

00:16:07.757 --> 00:16:11.767
every Record in the month, grouped by day, with each day’s total.

00:16:12.633 --> 00:16:18.285
Click any part of a chart on the Overview, and Monthly Records opens on just those Records.

00:16:19.167 --> 00:16:23.557
And at the bottom of the Overview, Review this month links to anything that needs you:

00:16:23.937 --> 00:16:30.639
Pending Records, Pending Statements, and spending that isn’t in any Monthly Budget, so no plan is tracking it.

00:16:32.267 --> 00:16:34.028
Budgets for trips and events

00:16:34.628 --> 00:16:36.908
Everything so far works month by month.

00:16:37.248 --> 00:16:40.268
A Budget, with no Monthly in front, is different:

00:16:40.568 --> 00:16:44.788
it has its own start and end, like a week in Bali, or a wedding.

00:16:45.178 --> 00:16:48.961
You see the whole trip in one place, even when it spans two months.

00:16:49.833 --> 00:16:52.722
Create one with a name, an amount and its dates.

00:16:53.433 --> 00:16:59.553
Automatic attach adds every Record dated inside the period, including income and transfers.

00:16:59.973 --> 00:17:04.708
To choose the Records yourself, turn it off, and attach them from the Budget’s page.

00:17:05.567 --> 00:17:08.687
A Budget is its own small dashboard for that period:

00:17:08.987 --> 00:17:11.707
what you’ve spent, what’s left, and your pace.

00:17:12.087 --> 00:17:15.361
Its Records still count on your monthly Dashboard as usual.

00:17:16.967 --> 00:17:18.560
Backups and Google Drive

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:23.650
You’ve put real work into explaining your money, so it’s worth knowing where it lives.

00:17:24.070 --> 00:17:28.090
Finpoint runs in your browser, and keeps your data on this device.

00:17:28.490 --> 00:17:34.375
There’s no Finpoint database, and your finances never pass through our servers, so we never see them.

00:17:35.233 --> 00:17:38.853
That means keeping a copy is up to you, and there are two ways.

00:17:39.253 --> 00:17:41.013
The first is a backup file.

00:17:41.203 --> 00:17:47.303
Download it from the Data page whenever you like, and restore it later, on this device or a new one.

00:17:47.743 --> 00:17:51.111
Restoring replaces this browser’s data with the backup.

00:17:51.967 --> 00:17:53.817
The second is Google Drive.

00:17:54.127 --> 00:18:01.899
Connect it once, and Finpoint keeps a copy in a hidden folder in your own Drive, automatically, so your devices stay in step.

00:18:02.767 --> 00:18:12.824
If the same data changed on two devices, Finpoint asks which copy to keep, and keeps that whole copy, never a mix of both, so your Records always add up.

00:18:13.700 --> 00:18:17.078
Either way, your data only goes to places you control.

00:18:18.700 --> 00:18:19.722
Your routine

00:18:20.322 --> 00:18:22.032
Here’s a routine that works.

00:18:22.202 --> 00:18:24.342
Import your latest bank exports.

00:18:24.562 --> 00:18:27.782
Fix any duplicates, and explain what’s new in Workflows.

00:18:28.082 --> 00:18:29.432
Check anything Pending.

00:18:29.662 --> 00:18:31.092
Glance at your Dashboard.

00:18:31.162 --> 00:18:32.285
And keep a backup.

00:18:33.167 --> 00:18:36.547
With Google Drive connected, that happens automatically.

00:18:36.927 --> 00:18:40.047
If not, download a backup file every so often.

00:18:40.900 --> 00:18:44.210
And whenever you’re unsure, choose Help at the top of any page.

00:18:44.580 --> 00:18:47.698
Every chapter of this guide is there, step by step.
